WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
Key management fails quietly until something goes wrong.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:16.760
Logs, traces, metrics, error reports, support bundles, screenshots, crash dumps, CI/CD output, and debugging tools can all leak key material or sensitive context.

3
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:30.060
Do not log symmetric keys, private keys, HMAC keys, JWT signing secrets, decrypted secrets, PEM blocks, JWK private values, connection strings, or full environment variables.

4
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:35.380
Redaction should happen before sensitive data leaves the application boundary where possible.

5
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:48.440
Monitoring should detect unusual key access, failed decryption spikes, signature verification failures, unexpected key versions, excessive secret reads, rotation failures, and use of retired keys.

6
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:54.140
These signals help distinguish ordinary failures from possible exposure, drift, or abuse.

7
00:00:54.420 --> 00:01:05.700
Tests should cover key loading, missing keys, wrong keys, old key versions, rotation behavior, decryption failure, signature verification failure, and safe error handling.

8
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.960
Tests must not use production keys or production secret stores.

9
00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:17.180
Good tests prove that failure paths are controlled and that key changes can happen without improvising during an incident.

10
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:31.220
Incident response should include revocation, rotation, blast-radius analysis, log review, backup review, exposed-data analysis, and validation that compromised keys no longer work.

11
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:40.280
Key-related incidents should improve future design through better ownership, shorter lifetimes, narrower access, stronger redaction, and tested rotation.

12
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:47.100
The team should know which key version protected which data and what to do when key material appears somewhere it should not.
